{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Mr. ___, I’m Dr. ___. You’re in the recovery area after your procedure. You had a radiofrequency ablation for one of the liver tumors, but there was some bleeding afterward. We did a scan that showed blood leaking near the ablation site, so you were taken to angiography to check for the source."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Bleeding? Is it serious? I don’t feel any pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It was concerning at the time. You became a bit hypotensive, so we gave you medication to support your blood pressure and briefly placed a breathing tube to keep you stable. But the good news is, the angiogram didn’t show any active bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So they couldn’t find where it was coming from?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The interventional radiologist checked the hepatic artery and even the adrenal and phrenic arteries nearby, but no active extravasation was seen. That means the bleeding likely stopped on its own or was sealed off when the probe was removed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember the last time I had a procedure, it went smoothly. Why did this happen now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "During the ablation of the tumor in segment 7, the probe was very close to the adrenal gland. That caused a temporary spike in blood pressure, which the anesthesia team managed. When the probe was pulled out, that’s when we saw signs of bleeding on the CT. To be safe, we stopped the rest of the procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the other tumors weren’t treated?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2159-03-03 20:58:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s right. We focused on controlling the bleeding. The segment 7 tumor was successfully ablated, but the two in segment 8 weren’t touched. We’ll discuss next steps with your oncology team in follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2159-03-03 21:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’m glad it’s under control. What about my blood pressure now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2159-03-03 21:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well now. Your blood pressure has been stable since we weaned off the pressors. We’re monitoring you closely overnight just to be safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs this morning show your hemoglobin is stable at 10.8, and liver enzymes are a bit elevated but expected after the ablation. We’re planning to discharge you today if you continue to do well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good to hear. Should I expect more pain or bleeding at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You might have some soreness at the rib site where the probe went in. We’re giving you a small supply of oxycodone for breakthrough pain, but avoid it if you’re drowsy or confused. Watch for increasing pain, fever, or dizziness—those would be reasons to call or come back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my usual medications? I take furosemide and spironolactone for my liver."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We held those overnight because of the blood pressure concerns, but you can restart them today as prescribed. All your other medications—nadolol, aspirin, omeprazole, glyburide—are back on schedule."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And when do I follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll see hepatology and oncology in about four weeks for imaging to check the ablation site and plan further treatment. Also, please get a repeat CBC as an outpatient to monitor your blood count."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2159-03-04 08:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You did well through a complicated situation. We’ll make sure your care team has all the details. You’re cleared to go home when you’re ready."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2159-03-03 20:37:00",
    "admission_end": "2159-03-04 13:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mr. ___, a male with hepatitis C-related cirrhosis and multifocal hepatocellular carcinoma, was admitted after experiencing post-procedural bleeding following a radiofrequency ablation (RFA) of a liver tumor in segment 7. A CT scan revealed arterial extravasation from the ablation tract, prompting transfer to the angiography suite. Angiography showed no active bleeding, and the patient was briefly hypotensive, requiring pressors and temporary intubation before stabilization. The procedure was terminated without treating two additional tumors in segment 8. He remained hemodynamically stable overnight with no further bleeding. His diuretics were held initially but restarted at discharge, and home medications were continued. He was discharged home the following day with instructions to monitor for complications and follow up with oncology and hepatology in four weeks for treatment reassessment.",
    "problems": [
      "Hemorrhage complicating a procedure",
      "Malignant neoplasm of liver, primary",
      "Esophageal varices without mention of bleeding",
      "Other iatrogenic hypotension",
      "Unspecified viral hepatitis C without hepatic coma",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Aortocoronary bypass status",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled"
    ]
  }
}
